      "title": "Sentence 1 — Describing Multiple Causes",
      "text": "**Original (D):**\n\n> Poverty, distance from school and care responsibilities can overlap, and each difficulty may compound the others.\n\n**Simpler paraphrase:**\n\nFinancial problems, long journeys to school and family duties may happen together and make one another worse.\n\n**Structure and grammar:**\n\nThe sentence combines two independent clauses using **and**.\n\n- Clause 1: *Poverty, distance from school and care responsibilities can overlap.*\n- Clause 2: *Each difficulty may compound the others.*\n\nThe first clause establishes coexistence. The second establishes interaction.\n\n*Can* and *may* indicate possibility rather than certainty. The word *each* treats the difficulties individually, while *the others* refers to the remaining difficulties.\n\nThis structure is particularly useful for explaining complex problems with interacting causes.\n\n**Transfer example:**\n\n> Staff shortages, unrealistic deadlines and poor planning can overlap, and each problem may compound the others.",
