      "text": "**Original (F):**\n\n> A young mother may be entitled to re-enter school yet still have no affordable childcare, safe transport or suitable sanitation facilities.\n\n**Simpler paraphrase:**\n\nA young mother may have the legal right to return to school but lack the support necessary to attend.\n\n**Structure and grammar:**\n\nThe sentence uses the contrastive pattern:\n\n**may + verb phrase + yet still + verb phrase**\n\nThe word *yet* introduces a surprising or important contrast, while *still* emphasises that the difficulty remains.\n\nNotice that both parts share the same subject and modal verb:\n\n- A young mother **may be entitled to** re-enter school.\n- A young mother **may still have** no affordable childcare.\n\nThe compact structure avoids repeating unnecessary words.\n\n**Transfer example:**\n\n> An employee may be entitled to flexible working hours yet still hesitate to request them because of workplace expectations.",
